m? A method to determine the contact patterns of adults that may lead to the spread of airborne infections</article-title><source>Proc Bio Sci</source><volume>264</volume><fpage>949</fpage><lpage>957</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9263464</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pmed-0050074-b022"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Edmunds</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name><name><surname>Kafatos</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Wallinga</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Mossong</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Mixing patterns and the spread of close-contact infectious diseases</article-title><source>Emerg Themes Epidemiol</source><volume>3</volume><fpage>10</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16907980</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pmed-0050074-b023"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Beutels</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Shkedy</surname><given-names>Z</given-names></name><name><surname>Aerts</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Van Damme</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Social mixing patterns for transmission models of close contact infections: exploring self-evaluation and diary-based data collection through a web-based interface</article-title><source>Epidemiol Infect</source><volume>134</volume><fpage>1158</fpage><lpage>1166</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16707031</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pmed-0050074-b024"><citation citation-type="other"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mikolajczyk</surname><given-names>RT</given-names></name><name><surname>Akmatov</surname><given-names>MK</given-names></name><name><surname>Rastin</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Kretzschmar</surname><give